== mediawiki.el -- Edit mediawiki sites from emacs ==

This version of mediawiki.el represents a merging of wikipedia-mode.el (maintained by Uwe Brauer) from [http://www.emacswiki.org/emacs/wikipedia-mode.el EmacsWiki] for its font-lock code, menu, draft mode, replying and convenience functions to produce mediawiki.el 2.0.

The package has been reorganized into a modular structure for better maintainability and code organization, while maintaining full backward compatibility.

=== Features ===

* Read and edit pages on any MediaWiki site over the API, with syntax highlighting and a ''MediaWiki'' menu.
* <code>mediawiki-file-mode</code> for editing MediaWiki markup in local files.
* Revision history browser (<code>mediawiki-history</code>) with per-revision view, diffs against the parent or the current revision, restore and browse.
* Watchlist and user-contributions browsers.
* Discussion page browser with reply and new-thread support; replies are indented relative to the comment being answered.  Includes a support desk wrapper.  See [https://github.com/hexmode/mediawiki-el/blob/master/DISCUSSION-TOOLS.mediawiki DISCUSSION-TOOLS.mediawiki] for how to use it, and [https://github.com/hexmode/mediawiki-el/blob/master/VILLAGE-PUMP.md VILLAGE-PUMP.md] for a walk-through at the Wikipedia Village Pump.
* <code>completion-at-point</code> for <code>[[wiki links]]</code>.
* Password/clientlogin authentication with auth-source support, and OAuth 2.0.

=== Installation ===

mediawiki.el requires Emacs 28.1 or later.

If you use [http://melpa.org/#/mediawiki MELPA], you can install via the M-x package-list-packages interface. This is preferable as you will have access to updates automatically.

Alternatively, if you have emacs 30+ you can use:

<syntaxhighlight lang="Emacs Lisp">
(use-package mediawiki
  :vc (:url "https://github.com/hexmode/mediawiki-el" :rev :latest))
</syntaxhighlight>

Otherwise, just make sure this file is in your load-path (usually ~/.emacs.d is included) and put (require 'mediawiki) in your ~/.emacs or ~/.emacs.d/init.el file.

'''Note:''' The modular structure is completely transparent to users. Simply require 'mediawiki as before and all functionality will be available. The reorganization maintains full backward compatibility.

==== Configuration and use ====

 M-x customize-group RET mediawiki RET
 M-x mediawiki-site RET Wikipedia RET

Open a wiki page:        M-x mediawiki-open
Save a wiki page:        C-x C-s  (mediawiki-save)
Save as another page:    C-x C-w  (mediawiki-save-as)
Save and bury the buffer: C-c C-c  (mediawiki-save-and-bury)
Reload from the wiki:    M-g      (mediawiki-reload)
Open the page in a browser: M-x mediawiki-browse
Browse revision history: M-x mediawiki-history (from a page buffer, or M-x mediawiki-history RET ''Page title'' RET)
Reply to a discussion:   C-c C-r  (mediawiki-reply-at-point-simple)
Insert wiki markup:      C-c C-f C-b (bold), C-c C-f C-i (italics), C-c C-f C-l (link), C-c C-f C-e (header), C-c C-w (signature), M-RET (numbered list), C-\ (bulleted list)
Open the page linked at point: C-RET  (mediawiki-open-page-at-point)

==== Basic usage: writing a page in a local file ====

You do not need to log in, or even be online, to write wikitext ([[https://github.com/hexmode/mediawiki-el/issues/30 issue #30]]).

Any buffer visiting a file ending in <code>.wiki</code> or <code>.mediawiki</code> is put in <code>mediawiki-file-mode</code>.  It derives from <code>mediawiki-mode</code>, so all the markup highlighting, the ''MediaWiki'' menu, and the insertion commands are available, but the save and open keys work on the local file:

<pre>
 C-x C-s    save the file          (save-buffer)
 C-x C-w    write to another file  (write-file)
 C-c C-c    save the buffer and close its window
 C-c C-o    find-file
 M-g        no wiki reload here; the standard M-g prefix still works
</pre>

So to write a page called "Some Topic" for pasting into the wiki's web editor:

# <code>C-x C-f ~/drafts/some_topic.wiki RET</code>
# Write the wikitext.
# <code>C-x C-s</code> to save it.
# Copy the buffer contents into the wiki's own editor when you are ready to publish.

For a file with a different extension, <code>M-x mediawiki-mode</code> gives you the same editing commands, though its <code>C-x C-s</code> tries to save to the wiki; use <code>M-x mediawiki-file-mode</code> if you want the file-oriented keys, or <code>C-x C-w</code> to write the buffer to a file.

<code>mediawiki-draft</code> is a lighter-weight alternative for collecting text:

* <code>M-x mediawiki-draft</code> opens a temporary buffer in <code>mediawiki-mode</code>.
* <code>M-x mediawiki-draft-buffer</code> appends the current buffer to <code>mediawiki-draft-data-file</code> (default <code>"~/Wiki/discussions/draft.wiki"</code>).
* <code>M-x mediawiki-draft-view-draft</code> visits that file.
* <code>M-x mediawiki-draft-send</code> copies a filed draft back into a buffer.

==== Logging in ====

Password-based login (bot passwords) works, but OAuth 2.0 is the preferred method; see [[#OAuth 2.0 Setup]].  If the password field for a site is empty, mediawiki.el falls back to the auth-source library, so you can keep credentials in <code>~/.authinfo.gpg</code> instead of your init file.

==== About Security ====

Although there is a place to put your password when you customize this package, there is no need to store your password in plain text.

If mediawiki sees that the password is empty, then it will use [https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_mono/auth.html the auth-source library] to retrieve the password.  This will allow you to use encryption to store your passwords.

=== Discussion tools ===

<code>mediawiki-discussion-tools</code> browses a discussion page as a list of threads: read them in a window below the list, reply, start a new one, mark one resolved, and search the page and its archives.  It works with any wikitext discussion page - talk pages, noticeboards, village pump desks - and <code>M-x mediawiki-support-desk</code> is a shortcut for the mediawiki.org support desk.

<pre>
 M-x mediawiki-discussion-tools RET Page name RET

 RET      open the thread at point        r      reply
 n / p    next / previous thread          m / C  start a new thread
 g        refresh the list                d      mark resolved
 s        search page and archives        q      close the thread view
</pre>

See [https://github.com/hexmode/mediawiki-el/blob/master/DISCUSSION-TOOLS.mediawiki DISCUSSION-TOOLS.mediawiki] for the full guide - what the status icons and priority tiers mean, the compose buffer, and the options - and [https://github.com/hexmode/mediawiki-el/blob/master/VILLAGE-PUMP.md VILLAGE-PUMP.md] for a tested walk-through at Wikipedia.

=== Package Structure ===

The mediawiki.el package is organized into the following modular components:

* '''mediawiki.el''' - Main entry point with package metadata and requires
* '''mediawiki-core.el''' - Core variables, constants, and fundamental utilities
* '''mediawiki-site.el''' - Site configuration and management
* '''mediawiki-faces.el''' - Font-lock face definitions for MediaWiki syntax
* '''mediawiki-font-lock.el''' - Font-lock keywords and syntax highlighting rules
* '''mediawiki-http.el''' - HTTP utilities (<code>url-http-get</code>, <code>url-http-post</code>) and compatibility functions
* '''mediawiki-api.el''' - MediaWiki API interaction and data processing
* '''mediawiki-auth.el''' - Password/clientlogin authentication
* '''mediawiki-oauth.el''' - OAuth 2.0 authentication
* '''mediawiki-page.el''' - Page editing, saving, and content management
* '''mediawiki-mode.el''' - Major mode definition, user interface, and <code>mediawiki-file-mode</code> for local files
* '''mediawiki-draft.el''' - Draft functionality and temporary editing
* '''mediawiki-completion.el''' - <code>completion-at-point</code> for <code>[[wiki links]]</code> using the prefixsearch API
* '''mediawiki-history.el''' - Revision history browser (<code>tabulated-list-mode</code>)
* '''mediawiki-diff.el''' - Revision diffs, ediff, and follow-mode helpers
* '''mediawiki-watchlist.el''' - Watchlist browser
* '''mediawiki-user.el''' - User contributions
* '''mediawiki-discussion-tools.el''' - Discussion page thread browser and replies
* '''mediawiki-support-desk.el''' - Support desk wrapper around <code>mediawiki-discussion-tools</code>
* '''mediawiki-utils.el''' - General utility functions

This modular structure improves code organization and maintainability while preserving all existing functionality and maintaining full backward compatibility.

=== OAuth 2.0 Setup ===

OAuth 2.0 is the preferred way to authenticate with MediaWiki sites. Password-based login (bot passwords) is deprecated on Wikimedia wikis and may not be available on others.

==== Registering an OAuth consumer ====

To use OAuth with a Wikimedia wiki, you need to register a consumer application:

# Visit https://meta.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:OAuthConsumerRegistration/propose/oauth2
# Fill in the fields:
#* '''Application name:''' <code>mediawiki.el</code>
#* '''Consumer version:''' <code>1.0</code>
#* '''Application description:''' A short description of your use
#* Check '''This consumer is only for use by USERNAME''' (replace USERNAME with your wiki username) to create an owner-only consumer — this is the simplest option
# Scroll down to '''Applicable grants''' and select the permissions you need (e.g., Edit existing pages, Edit protected pages, Edit your user CSS/JSON/JavaScript, Edit the MediaWiki namespace and sitewide/user JSON)
# Read and accept the terms, then click '''Propose consumer'''
# On the next page you will see your '''Client application key''', '''Client application secret''', and '''Access token'''

==== Storing OAuth credentials ====

Once you have your OAuth consumer credentials, configure mediawiki.el to use them.  You can either:

* Use the interactive command: <kbd>M-x mediawiki-oauth-setup-site</kbd>
* Or add the properties directly to <code>mediawiki-site-alist</code> in your Emacs init file:

<syntaxhighlight lang="Emacs Lisp">
(add-to-list 'mediawiki-site-alist
             '("Wikipedia"
               "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/"
               "username"              ;; Falls back to auth-source if empty
               ""                      ;; Leave password empty when using OAuth
               ""
               :description "English Wikipedia"
               :oauth-client-id "YOUR_CLIENT_APPLICATION_KEY"
               :oauth-client-secret "YOUR_CLIENT_APPLICATION_SECRET"
               :oauth-access-token "YOUR_ACCESS_TOKEN"))
</syntaxhighlight>

To avoid storing secrets in plain text, you can omit <code>:oauth-client-secret</code> and <code>:oauth-access-token</code> from your config and store them in [https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_mono/auth.html auth-source] instead (e.g., in <code>~/.authinfo.gpg</code>):

<syntaxhighlight lang="text">
machine en.wikipedia.org login YOUR_CLIENT_APPLICATION_KEY password YOUR_CLIENT_APPLICATION_SECRET port mediawiki-client-secret
machine en.wikipedia.org login YOUR_CLIENT_APPLICATION_KEY password YOUR_ACCESS_TOKEN port mediawiki-access-token
</syntaxhighlight>

With the client credentials and access token configured, mediawiki.el will automatically use OAuth 2.0 Bearer token authentication for all API requests to this site.

